# aarch64 boot data
# Stack and basic state are set up by the ELF patcher (scripts/tool/patch_aarch64.py)
# which inserts SP initialization before the Cyrius entry point.
# QEMU virt: RAM at 0x40000000, PL011 UART at 0x09000000

# ⛔ NO tsc_per_us / tsc_base HERE, and the reason is worth writing down because it was got WRONG once.
# The 1.56.19 GPU timing retune added both to this file, on the belief that core/gpu.cyr is included
# unconditionally and therefore compiles for aarch64. IT IS NOT. agnos.cyr has TWO separate
# `#ifdef ARCH_X86_64` blocks — one at lines 7-24 and a second at 73-127 — and core/gpu.cyr (:99),
# core/hda.cyr (:97) and core/main.cyr (:124) all sit inside the SECOND one. The aarch64 build pulls in
# exactly one file, arch/aarch64/main.cyr (:130). The misreading was seeing the `#endif` at line 24 and
# assuming everything below it was unguarded.
#
# ⚠ So when adding a symbol here to satisfy a core/ consumer, CHECK WHICH BLOCK THE CONSUMER IS IN
# FIRST. aarch64/stubs.cyr does supply an rdtsc() over CNTVCT_EL0, which makes "the timing code compiles
# on this arch" look plausible — it is not evidence, because that stub exists for the aarch64 tree's own
# callers. The declarations were removed again as dead: nothing on the aarch64 path referenced them.
